// @test: typecheck-pass
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: name-resolution, types, stdlib, collision
//
// Pin (T0993) — a file's own type declaration owns its name, even when the
// standard library declares that name too.
//
// Three lines, standalone file, before the fix:
//
//     public type TimeoutError is ();
//     fn make() -> TimeoutError { TimeoutError }
//
//     error<E400>: Type mismatch: expected 'TimeoutError',
//                                 found 'SelectTimeoutError'
//
// One identifier, two meanings, in ONE file: the return ANNOTATION resolved
// to the local declaration, and the constructor in VALUE position resolved
// to `core/async/select.vr`'s same-named alias, whose target is
// `SelectTimeoutError`.
//
// The eviction that should have prevented this exists — user-code phase
// wipes a stdlib binding sitting on a name the user declares — but it
// listed only two shapes — the Function case and the Variant case.
//
// A UNIT type's constructor is bound as `Named`, so the closed set walked
// past it. A RECORD body never hit this, because a record's value is a
// literal and consults no such binding; that is why the defect looked
// arbitrary rather than systematic.
//
// SURFACE: 5677 public type names in `core/`, every one bare-visible
// without a mount, and 104 of them declared more than once inside `core/`
// itself (`Capability` four times; `Thread`, `Stopwatch`, `Context`,
// `PerfCounter`, `DeadlineTimer` three each). Until this fix, none of
// those names could be declared by a user — or by another stdlib file.
//
// FOUND from `core/async/timer.vr`, which declares its own
// `public type TimeoutError is ();` on line 337 and failed on line 395 at
// `Result.Err(TimeoutError)`. The library was colliding with itself.

public type TimeoutError is ();

public type Stopwatch is ();

fn make_timeout() -> TimeoutError {
    TimeoutError
}

fn make_stopwatch() -> Stopwatch {
    Stopwatch
}

fn main() {
    let _ = make_timeout();
    let _ = make_stopwatch();
    print("ok");
}
